  "result": "There is a peculiar hour in every city when night has ended but morning has not quite begun. The streets belong to delivery trucks, bakers, nurses returning home, and insomniacs walking without destinations. Windows glow one by one, not dramatically, but with the quiet confidence of people resuming their lives.\n\nI like to imagine the city breathing differently then. During the day, it speaks in horns, footsteps, advertisements, and hurried conversations. Before dawn, it communicates through smaller sounds: a broom brushing pavement, bottles settling in a crate, a kettle beginning to tremble. Even traffic lights perform for nearly empty intersections, changing colors with admirable patience.\n\nThis hour reminds me that much of the world is maintained invisibly. Bread appears on shelves because someone worked while others slept. Floors are cleaned, trains inspected, servers restarted, flowers arranged outside corner shops. Morning seems effortless only because countless hands prepare it in advance.\n\nPerhaps that is why dawn can feel hopeful without making promises. It does not erase yesterday or guarantee anything about tomorrow. It simply reveals what has been waiting: rooftops, trees, tired faces, unfinished work. Light touches all of it without judgment.\n\nIf I could preserve one daily ritual, it would be to notice this transition more often. To stand somewhere still, holding a warm cup, and watch the sky trade charcoal for blue. Nothing extraordinary would happen, except the ancient ordinary miracle of a world becoming visible again, and inviting us, quietly, to begin with it once more today together.",
